[Rolldown](https://rolldown.rs/) is a JavaScript bundler with a Rollup-compatible API. In a Rolldown monorepo, Nx builds and caches projects across your workspace and rebuilds only what a change affects.

The Nx Plugin for Rolldown infers build tasks from Rolldown configuration files and contains generators to set up Rolldown in a project.

## Requirements

The `@nx/rolldown` plugin supports the following package versions.

| Package    | Supported Versions |
| ---------- | ------------------ |
| `rolldown` | ^1.0.0             |

[Nx generators](/docs/features/generate-code) install the latest supported versions automatically when scaffolding new projects.

## Setting up @nx/rolldown

### Installation

{% aside type="note" title="Keep Nx Package Versions In Sync" %}
Make sure to install the `@nx/rolldown` version that matches the version of `nx` in your repository. If the version numbers get out of sync, you can encounter some difficult to debug errors. You can [fix Nx version mismatches with this recipe](/docs/kb/keep-nx-versions-in-sync).
{% /aside %}

In any Nx workspace, you can install `@nx/rolldown` by running the following command:

```shell
nx add @nx/rolldown
```

## Configure task inference

The `@nx/rolldown/plugin` plugin looks for these Rolldown configuration files:

- `rolldown.config.js`
- `rolldown.config.cjs`
- `rolldown.config.mjs`
- `rolldown.config.ts`
- `rolldown.config.cts`
- `rolldown.config.mts`

A configuration file is associated with a project when its directory also contains a `package.json` or `project.json`. Nx creates a cached build task that runs `rolldown -c` and depends on the same task in dependency projects. The task outputs come from the configuration: each entry's `output.dir`, or `output.file` and its sourcemap. A configuration that exports an array contributes the outputs of every entry.

[`@nx/js/typescript` creates the `typecheck` task](/docs/technologies/typescript/introduction#how-the-typescript-plugin-infers-tasks) instead of this plugin, and the `@nx/rolldown:init` generator registers that plugin when it's missing.

Configure the plugin in `nx.json`:

```json
// nx.json
{
  "plugins": [
    {
      "plugin": "@nx/rolldown/plugin",
      "options": {
        "buildTargetName": "build"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

| Option            | Type   | Default | Description                             |
| ----------------- | ------ | ------- | --------------------------------------- |
| `buildTargetName` | string | `build` | Name of the cached Rolldown build task. |

Use `include` and `exclude` glob patterns on the plugin entry to scope inference.
A [target defaults](/docs/reference/nx-json#target-defaults) `plugin` filter must use the exact
identifier `@nx/rolldown/plugin`.

## Using @nx/rolldown

### Add Rolldown to a project

Add a `rolldown.config.ts` to an existing project with the following command:

```shell
nx g @nx/rolldown:configuration my-project
```

The generated configuration bundles `src/main.ts` (or `src/index.ts`) for Node.js. Packages listed in `dependencies` stay out of the bundle and load from `node_modules` at runtime, so native addons and packages that use dynamic `require` keep working. Pass `--externalDependencies=false` to bundle them, `--format=cjs` for CommonJS output, or `--platform=browser` to bundle everything for the browser.

### Generate a Node application using Rolldown

Node applications, other than Fastify ones, build with Rolldown by default in workspaces that use TypeScript path aliases. In other workspaces, pass `--bundler=rolldown`:

```shell
nx g @nx/node:app apps/my-api --bundler=rolldown
```

## Set up CI for your Rolldown monorepo

In CI, Nx runs [`nx affected`](/docs/features/ci-features/affected) to rebuild and retest only the projects a change touches, and [caches](/docs/features/cache-task-results) results to skip repeated work.

For a complete pipeline, see [Set up CI](/docs/getting-started/setup-ci).
